  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 217 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: \n\nHigh clouds are in the process of increasing from west-to-east \nacross northern New York and Vermont this afternoon, associated with \nmid-level trough extending from northern Ontario southward across \nLake Huron, Lake Erie and into Ohio. PBL winds have turned southerly \nwith 1022mb sfc anticyclone now east of Cape Cod. Southerly winds \nshould remain moderate overnight with approaching frontal system and \nabsence of much radiational cooling overnight. Overnight lows will \ngenerally range from the low-mid 60s. With the increasing southerly \ngradient flow, a Lake Wind Advisory has been posted (see Marine \nsection below for tonight. The Lake Wind Advisory may be necessary \nthrough the morning hours on Monday as well. \n\nNot much in the way of mid-tropospheric height falls overnight, but \nsome low-level WAA in advance of the approaching front should be \njust enough to yield scattered rain showers overnight, especially \nafter 03Z or so. There is some marginal elevated CAPE for parcels \nlifted from 850-775mb per HRRR and NAM3 forecast soundings. As such, \nkept with the idea that there could be a few embedded convective \nelements with a few overnight rumbles of thunder possible. PoPs are \nhighest across central/nrn VT during the pre-dawn hours (70-80%) and \ngenerally 30-50% elsewhere. \n\nHeading into Monday...surface boundary progresses through slowly \nfrom west to east. If we get some breaks in the cloud cover, there \nis a chance for modest PBL destabilization by late morning/early \nafternoon. Consistent with this, the 12Z HRRR developed 500-800 J/kg \nSBCAPE during the peak heating hours Monday, that is relatively \nuncapped. Anticipating a separate broken line of showers - and \nperhaps a few embedded thunderstorms - to develop during the midday \nhours while drifting slowly ewd across central/ern VT by mid-late \nafternoon. Weak mid-level lapse rates and modest sfc-6km shear \nsuggest that if thunderstorms form, they are unlikely to become \nsevere. However, have shown a separate period of 60-70% PoPs to \naccount for this expected separate period of convective showers and \nstorms during Monday afternoon. High temperatures on Monday will be \nseasonable to slightly below average for mid-August, in the mid-\nupper 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Lingering showers will move out on Wednesday night and \nThursday as broad cyclonic flow slowly migrates eastward. After the \nsystem moves off, a period of drier weather looks to return for the \nlatter half of the week. Daytime temperatures increase towards \nclimatological normals into next weekend with highs in the 70s and \nlower 80s as well as overnight lows falling into the 40s and 50s. \nThere is the potential for another system to sweep through next \nweekend bringing some sustained showers. We'll continue to monitor \nthis potential as the event approaches.",
